WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.032
Now let’s move to our second project Causal Bench

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:07.833
After building ExperimentWiki we wanted to know how well frontier

00:00:07.843 --> 00:00:12.628
models can actually perform causal inference and experimentation tasks

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:17.768
For our first version we focus on well-specified causal questions

00:00:18.811 --> 00:00:23.803
We constructed 328 questions covering 16 topics

00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:26.731
These questions come from three sources

00:00:27.337 --> 00:00:32.095
First our own data-generating processes where we explicitly define

00:00:32.105 --> 00:00:35.163
the causal structure and know the underlying truth

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:39.691
Second questions adapted from real research datasets

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.075
And third questions taken from public causal benchmarks

00:00:44.645 --> 00:00:49.197
During testing we give the model a clear task description including

00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:52.883
the meaning of each column and the first five rows of the data

00:00:53.491 --> 00:00:57.927
The data is clean and the relevant assumptions are specified or follow

00:00:57.937 --> 00:00:59.436
a standard textbook setting

00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:03.953
However the model may still need to select the appropriate causal

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:04.908
inference method

00:01:05.478 --> 00:01:09.914
Importantly the model cannot access the full dataset or execute its

00:01:09.924 --> 00:01:10.637
own code

00:01:11.228 --> 00:01:15.137
Instead it produces an analysis script and a structured answer

00:01:15.686 --> 00:01:20.676
We then execute the script on hidden data and evaluate it using programmatic

00:01:20.686 --> 00:01:26.116
checks including identification method selection estimates intervals

00:01:26.202 --> 00:01:27.327
and diagnostics

00:01:27.895 --> 00:01:31.789
This helps us evaluate whether models can implement correct causal

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:34.922
analysis rather than simply guessing an answer

00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:40.789
Models generally perform very well on textbook causal concepts

00:01:41.446 --> 00:01:46.140
For example their average performance on causal graphs and identifiability

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.187
questions is around 97 to 98 percent

00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:56.056
However experiment design and diagnosis are more difficult with average

00:01:56.066 --> 00:01:59.421
pass rates of 74 and 77 percent

00:02:00.436 --> 00:02:02.477
Here are the results from eleven models

00:02:03.061 --> 00:02:07.279
We find that the strongest frontier models perform remarkably well

00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:13.719
The top five models achieve approximately 93 to 95 percent pass rates

00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:17.766
on well-specified tasks and their differences are not statistically

00:02:17.776 --> 00:02:18.721
distinguishable

00:02:19.311 --> 00:02:21.622
But several interesting patterns emerge

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:26.741
First weaker models often fail because of engineering issues rather

00:02:26.751 --> 00:02:29.487
than purely conceptual misunderstandings

00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:34.330
Around 40 percent of their failures involve crashing scripts truncated

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.663
outputs or incorrect formatting

00:02:37.215 --> 00:02:41.935
Second coding requirements create a larger gap between strong and

00:02:41.945 --> 00:02:42.992
weak models

00:02:43.536 --> 00:02:48.707
On tasks requiring scripts the top five models achieve around 90 percent

00:02:48.871 --> 00:02:51.594
compared with 65 percent for the others

00:02:52.186 --> 00:02:56.777
So our main takeaway is that knowing causal concepts is no longer

00:02:56.787 --> 00:03:00.926
the main challenge for frontier models Applying those concepts to

00:03:00.936 --> 00:03:02.951
actual analysis is harder

00:03:03.561 --> 00:03:07.520
And this motivates our next version of Causal Bench where models have

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.224
greater autonomy but must deal with more realistic problems including

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:17.174
noisy data data-quality issues and potential violations of assumptions

00:03:18.228 --> 00:03:20.242
Now let’s move to our third project

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:25.094
Many companies cannot directly deploy closed-source frontier models

00:03:25.244 --> 00:03:30.274
on sensitive internal data They often need locally deployed open models

00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:34.438
So we want to know whether post-training can improve their causal

00:03:34.448 --> 00:03:35.870
inference capabilities

00:03:36.895 --> 00:03:43.225
We start with Qwen3 . 8-27B and use three training stages continued

00:03:43.235 --> 00:03:46.899
pre-training supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning

00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:53.333
For continued pre-training we use text from DoWhy and EconML documentation

00:03:53.908 --> 00:03:59.954
However the initial corpus contains only around 77,000 tokens and

00:03:59.964 --> 00:04:03.280
we observe almost no downstream improvement

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.964
Next we perform supervised fine-tuning using approximately 13,800

00:04:09.974 --> 00:04:14.153
question-answer examples covering causal graphs full analysis cases

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.275
and tool use

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.424
Our in-domain performance improves substantially from around 0.59

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.022
to 0.87

00:04:23.603 --> 00:04:25.927
But the external results are much worse

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.420
Performance on CLadder drops by 0.30 and tool-calling performance

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:33.624
drops by 0.21

00:04:34.645 --> 00:04:36.904
We identify two problems

00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:42.842
First the model learns our training-data format too strongly reducing

00:04:42.852 --> 00:04:45.794
its ability to follow different output formats

00:04:46.353 --> 00:04:51.304
Second every tool-calling example in our training data contains exactly

00:04:51.314 --> 00:04:52.478
one tool call

00:04:53.049 --> 00:04:57.807
As a result the model learns a very narrow pattern It struggles when

00:04:57.817 --> 00:05:01.996
a task requires no tool calls or multiple tool calls

00:05:02.629 --> 00:05:07.774
Finally we try reinforcement learning using GRPO but observe almost

00:05:07.784 --> 00:05:09.553
no additional improvement

00:05:10.103 --> 00:05:14.359
A possible explanation is that the combination of LoRA a small learning

00:05:14.369 --> 00:05:18.780
rate and KL regularization restricts how much the model can change

00:05:19.799 --> 00:05:23.732
To address these problems we conduct a second independent training

00:05:23.742 --> 00:05:25.550
run which we call Line B

00:05:26.145 --> 00:05:30.724
We first expand the continued pre-training corpus to approximately

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.958
1.57 million tokens

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.445
During supervised fine-tuning we also introduce replay data from general-purpose

00:05:39.455 --> 00:05:42.836
tasks to help preserve existing capabilities

00:05:43.408 --> 00:05:46.324
However tool-calling performance still drops

00:05:47.353 --> 00:05:52.201
We then identify that our replay data contains too few examples of

00:05:52.211 --> 00:05:54.290
the missing tool-use patterns

00:05:54.853 --> 00:06:00.629
So we create approximately 1,600 targeted counterexamples including

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:05.218
cases requiring no tool calls and multiple parallel tool calls

00:06:05.799 --> 00:06:10.764
After two targeted patches tool-calling performance recovers substantially

00:06:11.043 --> 00:06:15.455
while our in-domain causal performance remains almost unchanged

00:06:16.007 --> 00:06:18.382
We also observe some recovery on CLadder

00:06:19.395 --> 00:06:22.761
The key lesson is that post-training can improve performance on the

00:06:22.771 --> 00:06:26.757
target domain but it can also damage existing capabilities

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:31.661
And importantly some of this damage comes from the training-data distribution

00:06:31.683 --> 00:06:36.119
and output-format patterns rather than necessarily from losing causal

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:36.867
knowledge

00:06:37.436 --> 00:06:41.782
So we need to evaluate models across multiple dimensions not just

00:06:41.792 --> 00:06:42.982
the target benchmark
